are employed to detect heteroscedasticity, including the Park Test, White's Test, and Breusch-Pagan Test. The Park Test assesses the relationship between squared residuals and independent variables, while White's Test is a non-parametric method that does not assume a specific functional form of heteroscedasticity. The Breusch-Pagan Test evaluates whether the variance of the residuals is related to the independent variables. Each of these tests plays a crucial role in identifying heteroscedasticity, allowing researchers to take corrective measures to ensure the validity of their regression analyses [Data: Relationships (1602, 1652, 1340)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"The role of John Fox in understanding heteroscedasticity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"John Fox is a prominent figure in econometrics, particularly known for his contributions to understanding heteroscedasticity and its implications for regression analysis. His work emphasizes the importance of testing for heteroscedasticity and provides econometricians with tools to detect and address this issue effectively. By clarifying the implications of heteroscedasticity, Fox has enhanced the rigor of empirical research, ensuring that conclusions drawn from regression analyses are valid and reliable [Data: Relationships (1726)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Heteroscedasticity and error variance\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Error variance is a critical concept in econometrics that quantifies the variability of the error terms in a regression model. Heteroscedasticity directly affects error variance, leading to implications for the efficiency of estimators. When heteroscedasticity is present, OLS estimators remain unbiased but are no longer efficient, resulting in biased standard errors and unreliable hypothesis tests. Understanding the relationship between heteroscedasticity and error variance is essential for econometricians to ensure the robustness of their analyses [Data: Relationships (1564)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Remedial measures for addressing heteroscedasticity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"To mitigate the effects of heteroscedasticity, several remedial measures can be employed. Common approaches include using robust standard errors, which adjust the standard errors of OLS estimates to account for heteroscedasticity, and transforming the dependent variable. Weighted least squares (WLS) regression is another method that gives different weights to observations based on the variance of their errors. These techniques are vital for ensuring valid inference in the presence of heteroscedasticity [Data: Relationships (2384)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"The significance of empirical methods in detecting heteroscedasticity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Empirical methods are often utilized to investigate the presence of heteroscedasticity in data. These methods involve analyzing the relationship between residuals and independent variables to identify patterns indicative of non-constant variance. By employing empirical techniques, researchers can enhance the reliability of their econometric analyses and ensure that their conclusions are well-founded. The application of empirical methods is crucial for addressing the challenges posed by heteroscedasticity in regression models [Data: Relationships (1611)].\"\n        }\n    ]\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "protected_material_code": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "protected_material_text": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}], "created": 1771167075, "model": "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "fp_f97eff32c5", "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1004, "prompt_tokens": 11199, "total_tokens": 12203, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 1024}}, "prompt_filter_results": [{"prompt_index": 0, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "jailbreak": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}]}, "input": {"messages": [{"content": "\nYou are an AI assistant that helps a human analyst to perform general information discovery. Information discovery is the process of identifying and assessing relevant information associated with certain entities (e.g., organizations and individuals) within a network.\n\n# Goal\nWrite a comprehensive report of a community, given a list of entities that belong to the community as well as their relationships and optional associated claims. The report will be used to inform decision-makers about information associated with the community and their potential impact. The content of this report includes an overview of the community's key entities, their legal compliance, technical capabilities, reputation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n65,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,\"**Heteroscedasticity**\n\nHeteroscedasticity is a statistical phenomenon encountered in regression analysis, characterized by the non-constant variance of error terms across observations. Specifically, it occurs when the variance of the errors, denoted as \\( \\text{Var}(u_i) \\), varies with different levels of the independent variable \\( X \\). This condition can be formally expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Var}(u_i | X) \\neq \\sigma^2\n\\]\n\nfor all observations \\( i \\), where \\( \\sigma^2 \\) is a constant variance. Heteroscedasticity is significant because it can lead to inefficient estimates of the regression coefficients and biased standard errors, which in turn affects hypothesis testing and the reliability of statistical inferences.\n\nThe presence of heteroscedasticity implies that the ordinary least squares (OLS) estimators remain unbiased but are no longer efficient. This inefficiency arises because the OLS method assumes homoscedasticity, where the variance of the errors is constant across all levels of the independent variable. When this assumption is violated, the OLS estimators do not achieve the minimum variance among the class of linear unbiased estimators, leading to inflated standard errors and unreliable confidence intervals.\n\nHeteroscedasticity can manifest in various forms, often related to the scale of the dependent variable or the nature of the independent variables. For instance, in a model where the dependent variable represents income, higher income levels may exhibit greater variability in errors compared to lower income levels, resulting in a pattern of increasing variance.\n\nThe implications of heteroscedasticity are critical in econometric analysis. If not addressed, it can lead to incorrect conclusions regarding the significance of predictors and the overall model fit. Therefore, detecting and correcting for heteroscedasticity is essential for ensuring the robustness of regression results.\n\nSeveral methods exist for detecting heteroscedasticity, including graphical analysis (such as residual plots) and formal statistical tests like the Breusch-Pagan test and the White test. If heteroscedasticity is detected, various remedial measures can be employed, such as transforming the dependent variable, using weighted least squares (WLS) regression, or applying robust standard errors to adjust for the non-constant variance.\n\nIn summary, heteroscedasticity is a crucial concept in regression analysis that refers to the variability of error terms not being constant across observations. Its presence can significantly impact the efficiency of OLS estimators and the validity of statistical tests, making it imperative for econometricians to identify and address this condition in their analyses.\",60\r\n1848,WEIGHTED LEAST-SQUARES REGRESSION,Weighted least-squares regression is a statistical method used to estimate the parameters of a regression model when there is heteroscedasticity in the data.,2\r\n1316,PARK TEST,\"The Park Test is a formal statistical method employed to detect heteroscedasticity in regression analysis. Heteroscedasticity refers to the circumstance in which the variance of the errors (residuals) in a regression model is not constant across all levels of the independent variables. This violation of the assumption of homoscedasticity can lead to inefficient estimates and biased statistical tests, making the Park Test a crucial tool for econometricians.\n\nThe Park Test operates by examining the relationship between the residuals of a regression model and the independent variables. Specifically, it involves regressing the squared residuals from an initial ordinary least squares (OLS) regression on the independent variables. The underlying hypothesis of the Park Test is that if heteroscedasticity is present, the squared residuals will exhibit a systematic relationship with the independent variables.\n\nMathematically, the Park Test can be expressed as follows:\n\n1. Estimate the initial regression model:\n   \\[\n   Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\ldots + \\beta_k X_k + u\n   \\]\n   where \\(Y\\) is the dependent variable, \\(X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k\\) are the independent variables, \\(\\beta_0, \\beta_1, \\ldots, \\beta_k\\) are the coefficients, and \\(u\\) is the error term.\n\n2. Calculate the residuals \\(\\hat{u}\\) from the OLS estimation.\n\n3. Regress the squared residuals \\(\\hat{u}^2\\) on the independent variables:\n   \\[\n   \\hat{u}^2 = \\gamma_0 + \\gamma_1 X_1 + \\gamma_2 X_2 + \\ldots + \\gamma_k X_k + v\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\gamma_0, \\gamma_1, \\ldots, \\gamma_k\\) are the new coefficients and \\(v\\) is the new error term.\n\n4. The null hypothesis (\\(H_0\\)) of the Park Test posits that there is no relationship between the squared residuals and the independent variables, indicating homoscedasticity. The alternative hypothesis (\\(H_a\\)) suggests that such a relationship exists, indicating heteroscedasticity.\n\nThe importance of the Park Test lies in its ability to provide a formal mechanism for identifying heteroscedasticity, which, if unaddressed, can lead to inefficient parameter estimates and invalid inference in regression analysis. By detecting heteroscedasticity, researchers can take corrective measures, such as using robust standard errors or transforming the dependent variable, to ensure the validity of their regression results.\n\nThe assumptions underlying the Park Test include the following:\n\n1. The initial regression model must be correctly specified, meaning that it should include all relevant variables and the functional form should be appropriate.\n2. The residuals from the initial regression should be independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) under the null hypothesis of homoscedasticity.\n\nViolating these assumptions can lead to misleading results. For instance, if the initial model is misspecified, the relationship between the squared residuals and the independent variables may not accurately reflect the presence of heteroscedasticity. Consequently, econometricians must ensure that their models are well-specified before applying the Park Test.\n\nIn summary, the Park Test is a vital statistical tool for detecting heteroscedasticity in regression models. By analyzing the relationship between residuals and independent variables, it helps econometricians identify potential issues that could compromise the integrity of their regression analyses.\",4\r\n1347,WHITE'S HETEROSCEDASTICITY TEST,White's heteroscedasticity test is a statistical test used to detect heteroscedasticity in regression models.,3\r\n1280,ERROR VARIANCE,\"**Error Variance**\n\nError variance is a critical concept in econometrics that refers to the variability of the error terms (or residuals) in a regression model. It is a measure of how much the observed values deviate from the values predicted by the model, thereby indicating the model's accuracy and reliability. Mathematically, the error variance is denoted as \\( \\sigma^2 \\) and is calculated as the average of the squared differences between the observed values \\( y_i \\) and the predicted values \\( \\hat{y}_i \\):\n\n\\[\n\\sigma^2 = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the number of observations. \n\nThe importance of error variance lies in its implications for the efficiency of the estimators used in regression analysis. High error variance can lead to less reliable estimates of the regression coefficients, which in turn affects the overall conclusions drawn from the model. Specifically, if the error variance is large, the confidence intervals for the estimated coefficients will be wider, indicating greater uncertainty about the true parameter values.\n\nError variance is closely related to several key assumptions in regression analysis, particularly the assumption of homoscedasticity. Homoscedasticity posits that the error terms have constant variance across all levels of the independent variable(s). When this assumption holds, the estimators are said to be efficient, meaning they have the smallest possible variance among all unbiased estimators. Conversely, if the error variance is not constant (a condition known as heteroscedasticity), it can lead to inefficient estimators and biased standard errors, which can mislead hypothesis testing and confidence interval construction.\n\nThe consequences of violating the assumption of homoscedasticity are significant. In the presence of heteroscedasticity, the ordinary least squares (OLS) estimators remain unbiased, but they are no longer efficient. This inefficiency can result in invalid statistical inferences, as the standard errors of the coefficients may be underestimated or overestimated, leading to incorrect conclusions about the significance of predictors.\n\nIn summary, error variance is a fundamental aspect of regression analysis that quantifies the variability of the error terms. It plays a crucial role in determining the reliability and efficiency of the model's estimates. Understanding and addressing error variance, particularly through the lens of homoscedasticity, is essential for accurate econometric modeling and inference.\",2\r\n1373,WHITE TEST,\"The White Test is a statistical procedure employed in regression analysis to identify the presence of heteroscedasticity, which refers to the condition where the variance of the errors is not constant across all levels of the independent variables. This test is particularly significant because heteroscedasticity can lead to inefficient estimates and biased standard errors, ultimately affecting hypothesis testing and the reliability of regression results.\n\nThe White Test operates under the premise that if heteroscedasticity is present, the squared residuals from a regression model will exhibit a systematic relationship with the independent variables. Unlike some other tests for heteroscedasticity, such as the Park Test, the White Test does not require the specification of a particular functional form for the heteroscedasticity. This characteristic makes it a more flexible option for researchers who may not have prior knowledge about the nature of the variance in their data.\n\nTo conduct the White Test, the following steps are typically followed:\n\n1. **Estimate the initial regression model**: The researcher first fits a standard ordinary least squares (OLS) regression model to the data.\n   \n2. **Obtain residuals**: The residuals from this regression are calculated.\n\n3. **Square the residuals**: The squared residuals are then computed to assess the variance.\n\n4. **Regress squared residuals**: A new regression is performed where the dependent variable is the squared residuals, and the independent variables are the original independent variables from the initial regression. This regression can also include cross-products of the independent variables to capture potential non-linear relationships.\n\n5. **Conduct hypothesis testing**: The test statistic is derived from the regression output, specifically from the R-squared value of this auxiliary regression. The null hypothesis posits that there is no heteroscedasticity, while the alternative hypothesis suggests its presence. The test statistic follows a chi-squared distribution with degrees of freedom equal to the number of independent variables included in the auxiliary regression.\n\nThe formula for the test statistic can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Test Statistic} = n \\cdot R^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the sample size and \\( R^2 \\) is the coefficient of determination from the auxiliary regression.\n\nThe assumptions underlying the White Test include the following:\n\n1. **Linearity**: The relationship between the dependent and independent variables should be linear.\n2. **Independence**: The observations should be independent of one another.\n3. **No perfect multicollinearity**: The independent variables should not be perfectly correlated.\n\nThe importance of these assumptions lies in their impact on the validity of the test results. If the assumptions are violated, particularly the linearity and independence assumptions, the test may yield misleading conclusions regarding the presence of heteroscedasticity.\n\nIn summary, the White Test is a robust tool for detecting heteroscedasticity in regression models without the need for specifying a particular form of the heteroscedasticity. Its flexibility and ease of implementation make it a popular choice among econometricians. However, researchers must remain vigilant about the underlying assumptions to ensure the integrity of their findings. The identification of heteroscedasticity through the White Test can lead to the adoption of corrective measures, such as using robust standard errors or transforming the model, thereby enhancing the reliability of regression analyses.\",2\r\n1395,JOHN FOX,\"**John Fox**\n\nJohn Fox is a prominent author recognized for his significant contributions to the field of econometrics, particularly in the areas of heteroscedasticity and regression analysis. His work has been influential in enhancing the understanding of how heteroscedasticity affects the validity of regression models, which is crucial for accurate statistical inference.\n\nHeteroscedasticity refers to the circumstance in which the variance of the errors in a regression model is not constant across all levels of the independent variable(s). This violation of the classical linear regression assumption can lead to inefficient estimates and biased standard errors, ultimately affecting hypothesis testing and confidence intervals. Fox's contributions have helped clarify the implications of heteroscedasticity, providing econometricians with tools to detect and address this issue effectively.\n\nIn regression analysis, the general form of a linear model can be expressed as:\n\n\\[ Y_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_{1i} + \\beta_2 X_{2i} + \\ldots + \\beta_k X_{ki} + \\epsilon_i \\]\n\nwhere \\( Y_i \\) is the dependent variable, \\( X_{ji} \\) are the independent variables, \\( \\beta_j \\) are the coefficients, and \\( \\epsilon_i \\) represents the error term. The assumption of homoscedasticity, which states that the variance of \\( \\epsilon_i \\) is constant across all observations, is critical for the validity of ordinary least squares (OLS) estimators.\n\nFox's work emphasizes the importance of testing for heteroscedasticity, which can be conducted using various statistical tests, such as the Breusch-Pagan test or the White test. The Breusch-Pagan test, for instance, assesses whether the variance of the errors is related to the independent variables. If heteroscedasticity is detected, it can be addressed through several methods, including transforming the dependent variable, using weighted least squares (WLS), or applying robust standard errors to obtain valid inference.\n\nThe consequences of ignoring heteroscedasticity are significant. If the assumption of constant variance is violated, OLS estimators remain unbiased but are no longer efficient, leading to incorrect standard errors and unreliable hypothesis tests. This can result in misleading conclusions about the relationships between variables, which is particularly critical in policy-making and economic forecasting.\n\nIn summary, John Fox's contributions to the understanding of heteroscedasticity and regression analysis have provided valuable insights into the complexities of econometric modeling. His work underscores the necessity of recognizing and addressing heteroscedasticity to ensure the reliability of regression results. By equipping econometricians with the knowledge and tools to detect and correct for this issue, Fox has played a vital role in advancing the rigor and accuracy of empirical research in economics and related fields.\",2\r\n1437,BASIC II,Basic II is a section in Gujarati's textbook that discusses fundamental concepts in econometrics.,2\r\n1287,TOMAKE,Tomake refers to the process of distinguishing between homoscedasticity and heteroscedasticity in econometric models.,1\r\n1291,DAVID HENDRY,David Hendry is an economist known for his work on econometrics and time series analysis.,1\r\n1315,PRAIS AND HOUTHAKKER,\"Prais and Houthakker conducted pioneering work on family budget studies, examining the relationship between consumption and income.\",1\r\n1317,FAMILY BUDGET STUDIES,,1\r\n1320,EMPIRICAL METHOD,\"An empirical method refers to a way of gaining knowledge through observation and experimentation, often used in statistical analysis.\",1\r\n1339,GOLD-FELD–QUANDT TEST,The Goldfeld-Quandt test is a statistical test used to detect heteroscedasticity in regression models.,1\r\n1340,BREUSCH–PAGAN–GODFREY TEST,The Breusch-Pagan-Godfrey test is a method for testing heteroscedasticity in regression analysis.,1\r\n1349,KOENKER–BASSETT TEST,The Koenker–Bassett test is a statistical test used to detect heteroscedasticity based on squared residuals.,1\r\n1350,BREUSCH–PAGAN TEST,The Breusch–Pagan test is a statistical test used to detect heteroscedasticity in a regression model.,1\r\n1355,RICHARD HARRIS,Richard Harris is an author who wrote about using cointegration analysis in econometric modeling.,1\r\n1364,GOLD-FELD QUANDT TEST,The Goldfeld-Quandt test is a statistical test used to detect heteroscedasticity in a regression model.,1\r\n1403,STATISTICAL METHODS FOR SOCIAL SCIENTISTS,A book authored by Eric A. Hanushek and John E. Jackson that discusses statistical methods applicable to social science research.,1\r\n1404,QUARTERLY FINANCIAL REPORT FOR MANUFACTURING CORPORATIONS,A report published by the Federal Trade Commission and the Securities and Exchange Commission that provides financial data on U.S. manufacturing industries.,1\r\n642,F-VALUE,\"**F-Value**\n\nThe F-value is a crucial statistic employed in both Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) and regression analysis, serving as a measure to compare the variance between groups to the variance within groups. It is primarily used in the context of the F-test, which assesses whether the variances of two or more populations are significantly different. The F-value is calculated as the ratio of the mean square between groups (MSB) to the mean square within groups (MSW), expressed mathematically as:\n\n\\[\nF = \\frac{MSB}{MSW}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( MSB \\) (Mean Square Between) is calculated as the sum of squares between groups divided by the degrees of freedom between groups.\n- \\( MSW \\) (Mean Square Within) is calculated as the sum of squares within groups divided by the degrees of freedom within groups.\n\nThe F-value plays a pivotal role in hypothesis testing, particularly in determining the statistical significance of the overall regression model or the differences among group means. A higher F-value indicates a greater disparity between group variances relative to the variances within groups, suggesting that at least one group mean is significantly different from the others.\n\nIn the context of regression analysis, the F-value is used to test the null hypothesis that all regression coefficients are equal to zero (i.e., \\( H_0: \\beta_1 = \\beta_2 = ... = \\beta_k = 0 \\)). If the calculated F-value exceeds the critical value from the F-distribution for a given significance level (commonly 0.05), the null hypothesis is rejected, indicating that the model explains a significant portion of the variance in the dependent variable.\n\nThe assumptions underlying the F-test include:\n1. **Independence**: The observations must be independent of each other.\n2. **Normality**: The data in each group should be approximately normally distributed.\n3. **Homogeneity of Variances**: The variances among the groups should be equal (homoscedasticity).\n\nThese assumptions are critical because violations can lead to inaccurate conclusions. For instance, if the assumption of homogeneity of variances is violated, the F-test may yield misleading results, potentially increasing the Type I error rate (rejecting a true null hypothesis). In such cases, alternative methods, such as the Welch's ANOVA, may be employed to account for unequal variances.\n\nThe F-value is not only significant in hypothesis testing but also in model selection and evaluation. In regression analysis, it helps in comparing nested models, where one model is a special case of another. The F-test can determine if the additional parameters in the more complex model significantly improve the fit compared to the simpler model.\n\nIn summary, the F-value is a fundamental statistic in econometrics, particularly in ANOVA and regression analysis, used to compare variances and assess the significance of group differences or model fit. Its calculation, assumptions, and implications are essential for conducting valid statistical tests and drawing reliable conclusions from data.\",5\r\n1004,CRITICAL F VALUES,Critical F values are thresholds used in hypothesis testing to determine whether to reject the null hypothesis based on the F-value.,1\r\n1319,GLEJSER TEST,\"The Glejser Test is a statistical method employed in regression analysis to identify the presence of heteroscedasticity, which refers to the condition where the variance of the errors is not constant across all levels of the independent variables. Heteroscedasticity can lead to inefficient estimates and biased statistical tests, making it crucial to detect and address in econometric modeling.\n\nThe primary purpose of the Glejser Test is to assess whether the variance of the residuals from a regression model is related to the values of the independent variables. This is particularly important because the presence of heteroscedasticity can invalidate standard inferential statistics, such as t-tests and F-tests, which assume homoscedasticity (constant variance of errors).\n\nThe methodology of the Glejser Test involves regressing the absolute values of the residuals obtained from an initial regression model on the independent variables. Mathematically, this can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n|e_i| = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\ldots + \\beta_k X_k + u_i\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( |e_i| \\) represents the absolute value of the residuals, \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k \\) are the independent variables, \\( \\beta_0, \\beta_1, \\ldots, \\beta_k \\) are the coefficients to be estimated, and \\( u_i \\) is the error term. If the coefficients of the independent variables in this regression are statistically significant, it indicates the presence of heteroscedasticity.\n\nThe assumptions underlying the Glejser Test include the following:\n\n1. **Linearity**: The relationship between the absolute residuals and the independent variables should be linear.\n2. **Independence**: The residuals should be independent of each other.\n3. **No perfect multicollinearity**: The independent variables should not be perfectly correlated.\n4. **Normality of errors**: While not strictly necessary for the test itself, the normality of errors is often assumed for the validity of inferential statistics.\n\nThe importance of the Glejser Test lies in its ability to provide a diagnostic check for heteroscedasticity, which, if present, can lead to inefficient parameter estimates and unreliable hypothesis tests. By identifying heteroscedasticity, researchers can take corrective measures, such as transforming variables or using robust standard errors, to ensure the validity of their regression results.\n\nIn summary, the Glejser Test is a valuable tool in econometrics for detecting heteroscedasticity by regressing the absolute values of residuals on independent variables. Its effectiveness hinges on the assumptions of linearity, independence, and the absence of multicollinearity. By addressing heteroscedasticity, researchers can enhance the reliability of their econometric analyses and ensure that their conclusions are well-founded.\",2\r\n1318,GOLDVELD AND QUANDT,\"Stephen M. Goldfeld and Richard E. Quandt are authors known for their work on nonlinear methods in econometrics, particularly regarding heteroscedasticity.\",1\r\n1361,WHITE'S PROCEDURE,White's procedure is a statistical method used to obtain heteroscedasticity-consistent standard errors in regression analysis.,4\r\n1362,APPENDIX 11A.4,Appendix 11A.4 outlines White's procedure for estimating heteroscedasticity-consistent variances and standard errors.,1\r\n1363,HETEROSCEDASTICITY-CONSISTENT VARIANCES,Heteroscedasticity-consistent variances are estimates that account for the presence of heteroscedasticity in regression analysis.,1\r\n1451,CROSS SECTION,\"**Cross Section**\n\nCross section refers to a type of data collected at a single point in time across multiple subjects or entities. This data structure is pivotal in econometric analysis as it allows researchers to examine and compare various characteristics or behaviors of different subjects simultaneously, providing a snapshot of the population under study.\n\nThe importance of cross-sectional data lies in its ability to facilitate the analysis of relationships between variables without the complications introduced by time dynamics. For instance, researchers can investigate how income levels, education, and employment status correlate among individuals within a specific year, thereby enabling the identification of patterns and trends that may inform policy decisions or economic theories.\n\nIn terms of its relationship to other data types, cross-sectional data contrasts with time series data, which involves observations collected over multiple time periods for the same subjects. While time series data is essential for analyzing trends and dynamics over time, cross-sectional data is particularly useful for understanding the variation among subjects at a specific moment, making it a valuable tool for descriptive and inferential statistics.\n\nThe analysis of cross-sectional data often employs various econometric methods, including regression analysis, to estimate relationships between dependent and independent variables. For example, a researcher might use a linear regression model to assess the impact of education on income, represented mathematically as:\n\n\\[ Y_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_i + \\epsilon_i \\]\n\nwhere \\( Y_i \\) is the dependent variable (income), \\( X_i \\) is the independent variable (education), \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_1 \\) is the coefficient representing the effect of education on income, and \\( \\epsilon_i \\) is the error term.\n\nWhen utilizing cross-sectional data, several assumptions must be considered to ensure the validity of the econometric analysis. These include:\n\n1. **Independence of Observations**: The observations should be independent of one another. This assumption is crucial because correlated observations can lead to biased estimates and incorrect inferences.\n\n2. **Linearity**: The relationship between the dependent and independent variables is assumed to be linear. If this assumption is violated, the model may not accurately capture the true relationship, leading to misinterpretation of results.\n\n3. **Homoscedasticity**: The variance of the error terms should be constant across all levels of the independent variable. If heteroscedasticity is present, it can result in inefficient estimates and affect hypothesis testing.\n\n4. **No Perfect Multicollinearity**: The independent variables should not be perfectly correlated with one another. Perfect multicollinearity makes it impossible to isolate the individual effect of each variable on the dependent variable.\n\n5. **Normality of Errors**: While not strictly necessary for estimation, the normality of error terms is important for conducting hypothesis tests and constructing confidence intervals.\n\nViolations of these assumptions can lead to biased estimates, inefficient results, and incorrect conclusions. For instance, if the independence assumption is violated, it may result in inflated standard errors, leading to unreliable hypothesis tests. Similarly, if homoscedasticity is not met, the estimates may still be unbiased but inefficient, resulting in wider confidence intervals.\n\nIn summary, cross-sectional data is a fundamental component of econometric analysis, providing a framework for understanding relationships among variables at a specific point in time. Its effective use hinges on adherence to key assumptions, which, if violated, can significantly impact the reliability of the analysis.\",2\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n40,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,OLS,\"**Heteroscedasticity and Ordinary Least Squares (OLS)**\n\nHeteroscedasticity refers to the circumstance in regression analysis where the variance of the errors is not constant across all levels of the independent variable(s). This phenomenon is particularly significant in the context of Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression, which is a widely used method for estimating the parameters of a linear regression model. \n\n**Impact on OLS Estimators**\n\nThe presence of heteroscedasticity affects the efficiency of OLS estimators. While OLS estimators remain unbiased in the presence of heteroscedasticity, they become inefficient, meaning that they do not achieve the minimum variance among the class of linear unbiased estimators. This inefficiency arises because the standard errors of the estimated coefficients are biased, leading to unreliable hypothesis tests. Specifically, the usual OLS standard errors are calculated under the assumption of homoscedasticity (constant variance of errors), and when this assumption is violated, the resulting statistical tests (such as t-tests and F-tests) may yield misleading conclusions.\n\n**Assumptions of OLS**\n\nOLS regression is predicated on several key assumptions, one of which is homoscedasticity. The assumption of homoscedasticity implies that the variance of the error terms is constant across all levels of the independent variable(s). When heteroscedasticity is present, it violates this assumption, which can lead to several consequences:\n\n1. **Inefficiency of Estimates**: The OLS estimators remain unbiased but are no longer the Best Linear Unbiased Estimators (BLUE). This means that while the estimates of the coefficients are correct on average, they do not have the smallest possible variance, which is crucial for making precise inferences.\n\n2. **Invalid Hypothesis Testing**: The standard errors of the coefficients are biased, which affects the reliability of hypothesis tests. This can lead to incorrect conclusions regarding the significance of predictors in the model.\n\n3. **Model Specification Issues**: Heteroscedasticity may indicate that the model is misspecified, suggesting that important variables may be omitted or that a nonlinear relationship exists between the independent and dependent variables.\n\n**Addressing Heteroscedasticity**\n\nTo mitigate the effects of heteroscedasticity, several approaches can be employed. One common method is to use robust standard errors, which adjust the standard errors of the OLS estimates to account for heteroscedasticity. Another approach is to transform the dependent variable or to use weighted least squares (WLS) regression, which gives different weights to observations based on the variance of their errors.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nIn summary, heteroscedasticity is a critical concept in econometrics that affects the efficiency and reliability of OLS estimators. While OLS estimators remain unbiased in the presence of heteroscedasticity, their inefficiency and the potential for invalid hypothesis testing underscore the importance of diagnosing and addressing this issue in regression analysis. Understanding the implications of heteroscedasticity and the assumptions underlying OLS is essential for conducting robust econometric analyses and drawing valid conclusions from empirical data.\",139\r\n2384,WEIGHTED LEAST-SQUARES REGRESSION,OLS,OLS is a foundational method that is adapted in weighted least-squares regression to account for heteroscedasticity,81\r\n1602,PARK TEST,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,\"The Park Test is a statistical method specifically designed to detect heteroscedasticity in regression models. Heteroscedasticity refers to the circumstance in which the variance of the errors in a regression model is not constant across all levels of the independent variable(s). This violation of the assumption of homoscedasticity can lead to inefficient estimates and biased statistical tests, ultimately affecting the reliability of the regression results.\n\nThe primary purpose of the Park Test is to formally assess the presence of heteroscedasticity in regression analysis. It operates under the premise that if heteroscedasticity is present, the variance of the error term can be modeled as a function of the independent variables. The test involves estimating a regression model and then examining the relationship between the fitted values (or the independent variables) and the squared residuals from the initial regression. \n\nMathematically, the Park Test can be expressed as follows:\n\n1. Estimate the original regression model:\n   \\[\n   Y_i = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_i + \\epsilon_i\n   \\]\n   where \\(Y_i\\) is the dependent variable, \\(X_i\\) represents the independent variable(s), \\(\\beta_0\\) and \\(\\beta_1\\) are the coefficients, and \\(\\epsilon_i\\) is the error term.\n\n2. Calculate the residuals \\(\\hat{\\epsilon}_i\\) from the regression.\n\n3. Regress the squared residuals \\(\\hat{\\epsilon}_i^2\\) on the independent variables \\(X_i\\):\n   \\[\n   \\hat{\\epsilon}_i^2 = \\gamma_0 + \\gamma_1 X_i + u_i\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\gamma_0\\) and \\(\\gamma_1\\) are coefficients to be estimated, and \\(u_i\\) is the new error term.\n\n4. The null hypothesis of the Park Test states that there is no relationship between the squared residuals and the independent variables, implying homoscedasticity. If the test statistic derived from this regression is significant, it suggests the presence of heteroscedasticity.\n\nThe assumptions underlying the Park Test include the following:\n\n1. The model is correctly specified, meaning that the functional form of the regression is appropriate.\n2. The errors are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) in the absence of heteroscedasticity.\n3. The independent variables are not perfectly multicollinear.\n\nThe importance of the Park Test lies in its ability to identify heteroscedasticity, which, if unaddressed, can lead to inefficient parameter estimates and invalid inference. When heteroscedasticity is present, standard errors of the coefficients may be biased, leading to unreliable hypothesis tests and confidence intervals.\n\nIf the Park Test indicates the presence of heteroscedasticity, several remedial measures can be taken. These include transforming the dependent variable, using weighted least squares (WLS) regression, or applying robust standard errors to correct for the heteroscedasticity.\n\nIn summary, the Park Test is a valuable tool in econometrics for detecting heteroscedasticity in regression models. By formally assessing the relationship between the squared residuals and the independent variables, it helps ensure the validity of regression analyses, thereby enhancing the reliability of econometric conclusions.\",64\r\n1652,WHITE'S HETEROSCEDASTICITY TEST,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,White's test is specifically designed to detect heteroscedasticity in regression models,63\r\n1564,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,ERROR VARIANCE,\"**Heteroscedasticity and Error Variance**\n\nHeteroscedasticity is a critical concept in regression analysis that refers to the condition where the error variance is not constant across all levels of the independent variable(s). In a well-specified regression model, it is assumed that the error terms, which represent the unexplained variation in the dependent variable, have a constant variance, known as homoscedasticity. However, when heteroscedasticity is present, the variability of the error terms changes, leading to implications for the validity of statistical inferences made from the model.\n\nThe error variance is a fundamental aspect of regression analysis, as it directly influences the efficiency of the estimators. In the presence of heteroscedasticity, the ordinary least squares (OLS) estimators remain unbiased and consistent, but they are no longer efficient. This inefficiency arises because the standard errors of the estimated coefficients can be biased, leading to unreliable hypothesis tests and confidence intervals. Specifically, the estimated standard errors may be underestimated or overestimated, which affects the t-statistics and p-values used to assess the significance of the predictors.\n\nThe presence of heteroscedasticity can be detected through various diagnostic tests, such as the Breusch-Pagan test or the White test. These tests evaluate whether the variance of the residuals is related to the independent variables. If heteroscedasticity is detected, it is essential to address it to ensure valid inference. Common remedies include transforming the dependent variable, using weighted least squares (WLS) regression, or applying robust standard errors that adjust for heteroscedasticity.\n\nThe implications of violating the assumption of constant error variance are significant. When heteroscedasticity is ignored, the OLS estimators may still provide point estimates that are close to the true parameter values, but the associated inferential statistics may lead to incorrect conclusions. For instance, researchers may incorrectly reject or fail to reject null hypotheses based on misleading p-values, which can have serious consequences in empirical research and policy-making.\n\nIn summary, heteroscedasticity is characterized by non-constant error variance in regression models, which poses challenges for the reliability of statistical inference. Understanding error variance is crucial for econometricians, as it underpins the assumptions of regression analysis and affects the efficiency of estimators. Addressing heteroscedasticity is vital for ensuring that the conclusions drawn from regression analyses are valid and reliable.\",62\r\n1682,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,WHITE TEST,\"**Heteroscedasticity and the White Test**\n\nHeteroscedasticity refers to a condition in regression analysis where the variance of the errors is not constant across all levels of the independent variable(s). This violation of the assumption of homoscedasticity can lead to inefficient estimates and biased statistical tests, ultimately affecting the reliability of the regression results. In the presence of heteroscedasticity, standard errors of the estimated coefficients may be biased, leading to incorrect conclusions regarding hypothesis tests and confidence intervals.\n\nThe importance of detecting heteroscedasticity lies in its potential to distort the results of regression analyses. If heteroscedasticity is present, ordinary least squares (OLS) estimators remain unbiased but are no longer efficient, meaning that they do not have the minimum variance among the class of linear unbiased estimators. Consequently, hypothesis tests based on these estimators may yield misleading results, prompting the need for robust standard errors or alternative estimation techniques.\n\nThe White Test is a statistical method specifically designed to detect the presence of heteroscedasticity in regression models. It is a non-parametric test that does not assume a specific functional form of the heteroscedasticity, making it a versatile tool for practitioners. The White Test involves estimating the original regression model and then regressing the squared residuals from this model on the original independent variables and their squares (and possibly cross-products). The test statistic is derived from this auxiliary regression.\n\nMathematically, the White Test can be summarized as follows:\n\n1. Estimate the original regression model:\n   \\[\n   Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\ldots + \\beta_k X_k + u\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate the residuals \\( \\hat{u} \\) and their squares \\( \\hat{u}^2 \\).\n\n3. Regress \\( \\hat{u}^2 \\) on the independent variables:\n   \\[\n   \\hat{u}^2 = \\gamma_0 + \\gamma_1 X_1 + \\gamma_2 X_2 + \\ldots + \\gamma_k X_k + v\n   \\]\n\n4. The test statistic is computed as:\n   \\[\n   nR^2\n   \\]\n   where \\( n \\) is the sample size and \\( R^2 \\) is the coefficient of determination from the auxiliary regression.\n\nThe null hypothesis of the White Test posits that there is no heteroscedasticity (i.e., the variance of the errors is constant). A significant test statistic (typically compared against a chi-squared distribution) leads to the rejection of the null hypothesis, indicating the presence of heteroscedasticity.\n\nThe assumptions underlying the White Test include the following:\n- The model is correctly specified, meaning that the functional form of the regression is appropriate.\n- The errors are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) under the null hypothesis of homoscedasticity.\n\nViolating these assumptions can lead to incorrect conclusions regarding the presence of heteroscedasticity. For instance, if the model is misspecified, the test may indicate heteroscedasticity when it is not present, or vice versa.\n\nIn summary, heteroscedasticity is a critical consideration in regression analysis, affecting the efficiency and reliability of estimators. The White Test serves as a robust method for detecting this issue, allowing researchers to address potential violations of the homoscedasticity assumption and improve the validity of their econometric models.\",62\r\n1726,JOHN FOX,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,John Fox discusses the implications of heteroscedasticity in regression analysis,62\r\n1787,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,BASIC II,\"Heteroscedasticity is discussed in the Basic II section of Gujarati's textbook, highlighting its importance in econometric analysis\",62\r\n1565,TOMAKE,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,\"The concept of heteroscedasticity is central to the event of Tomake, which aims to clarify differences in variance in econometric models\",61\r\n1567,DAVID HENDRY,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,David Hendry discusses the concept of heteroscedasticity in his econometric work,61\r\n1599,PRAIS AND HOUTHAKKER,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,Prais and Houthakker's work on family budget studies suggests that heteroscedasticity may be encountered in similar surveys,61\r\n1603,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,FAMILY BUDGET STUDIES,Family budget studies indicate that heteroscedasticity may be present due to varying consumption patterns across different income levels,61\r\n1611,EMPIRICAL METHOD,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,Empirical methods are often used to investigate the presence of heteroscedasticity in data,61\r\n1637,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,GOLD-FELD–QUANDT TEST,The Goldfeld-Quandt test is specifically designed to identify the presence of heteroscedasticity in regression models.,61\r\n1638,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,BREUSCH–PAGAN–GODFREY TEST,The Breusch-Pagan-Godfrey test is used to test for heteroscedasticity in regression analysis.,61\r\n1653,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,KOENKER–BASSETT TEST,The Koenker–Bassett test is used to identify heteroscedasticity in regression analysis,61\r\n1654,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,BREUSCH–PAGAN TEST,The Breusch–Pagan test is utilized to detect heteroscedasticity in regression models,61\r\n1657,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,RICHARD HARRIS,\"Richard Harris wrote about econometric modeling, including issues of heteroscedasticity\",61\r\n1668,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,GOLD-FELD QUANDT TEST,The Goldfeld-Quandt test is used to identify the presence of heteroscedasticity in regression models,61\r\n1740,STATISTICAL METHODS FOR SOCIAL SCIENTISTS,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,The book discusses the concept of heteroscedasticity in the context of statistical methods for social scientists,61\r\n1741,HETEROSCEDASTICITY,QUARTERLY FINANCIAL REPORT FOR MANUFACTURING CORPORATIONS,The report may provide data that can be analyzed for heteroscedasticity in manufacturing financial metrics,61\r\n1097,F-VALUE,MULTIPLE REGRESSION,The F-value is used in multiple regression to compare the fits of different models.,38\r\n1162,F-VALUE,CRITICAL F VALUES,Critical F values are compared against the F-value to determine the outcome of the hypothesis test,6\r\n1606,PARK TEST,GLEJSER TEST,Both the Park test and the Glejser test are methods used to detect heteroscedasticity in regression analysis,6\r\n1607,PARK TEST,GOLDVELD AND QUANDT,Goldfeld and Quandt are known for their critique and analysis of the Park test in the context of econometrics,5\r\n1670,WHITE'S PROCEDURE,APPENDIX 11A.4,Appendix 11A.4 provides details on how to implement White's procedure,5\r\n1671,WHITE'S PROCEDURE,HETEROSCEDASTICITY-CONSISTENT VARIANCES,White's procedure provides a method to calculate heteroscedasticity-consistent variances,5\r\n1795,CROSS SECTION,POOLED DATA,\"Pooled data incorporates both cross-sectional and time series elements, enhancing the analysis of economic relationships.\",4\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\nOutput:", "role": "user"}], "parameters": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "frequency_penalty": 0.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1, "presence_penalty": 0.0, "temperature": 0.0, "top_p": 1.0, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}}}, "key": "chat_create_community_report_86c5562068530a23730d33cade56e3d6dc0c839fbcfd106bf65e59f5f3dea55b_v2"}